<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Congress of Panama</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1826">1826</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Amphictyonic Congress</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Congress of Panama was a meeting of Spanish American republics called by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar">Simón Bolívar</a> and held in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama-city">Panama City</a> in June and July 1826 to form a league of the new states. Gran Colombia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federal-republic-of-central-america">Federal Republic of Central America</a> attended, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>' delegates arrived too late and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a> was not invited. Its treaty of perpetual union was ratified only by Gran Colombia.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/congress-of-panama.jpg" alt="Congress of Panama" width="320" height="289" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nagihuin · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Colombia_(Miranda)_y_Congreso_de_Panam%C3%A1_(Bol%C3%ADvar).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar">Simón Bolívar</a> called the congress from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> on 7 December 1824, and José Faustino Sánchez Carrión, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>'s Minister of Government and Foreign Relations, who shared his ideal of Hispanic American unity, sent the invitations. Its other name, the Amphictyonic Congress, honoured the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delphic-amphictyony">Amphictyonic League</a> of Ancient Greece. Its aim was a common policy of the new republics towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a>: it proposed a league with a shared military and diplomatic policy, a mutual defence pact and a plan to liberate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-rico">Puerto Rico</a>, but never the formation of a unitary state.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Chile and the United Provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a> declined to attend, out of mistrust of Bolívar's influence; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazilian-empire">Empire of Brazil</a> stayed away, expecting a hostile reception during its war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a> over present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruguay">Uruguay</a>; and isolationist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraguay">Paraguay</a> was not invited. Bolívar also invited the United Kingdom and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> as observers, for their commercial interests in Hispanic America.</p><p>The British observer, Edward James Dawkins, had orders from George Canning to seek trade agreements and to dissuade Gran Colombia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> from backing expeditions to free <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-rico">Puerto Rico</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> envoy, Jan van Veer, came to offer mediation between the republics and Spain, but lacked accreditation, and since the Dutch monarchy recognised none of the republics he was received only as an individual.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Bolívar's project soon foundered amid civil war in Gran Colombia, the disintegration of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a> and the rise of nationalism, while the United Kingdom secured many favourable trade deals at the congress. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, President John Quincy Adams and Secretary of State Henry Clay wanted to take part, but politicians from the Southern United States, wary of republics that had mostly outlawed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, held up the funds and the confirmation of the delegates. Richard C. Anderson Jr. died on the way to Panama, and John Sergeant arrived only after the discussions had ended.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Congress of Panama” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1826 he convened the <b>Congress of Panama</b>, the first attempt at a league of Spanish American republics; only Gran Colombia ratified its treaties, but it is often cited as a forerunner of later inter-American cooperation.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gran-colombia">Gran Colombia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Its support for Simón Bolívar helped the liberation of Venezuela and northern South America, and Pétion's request that Bolívar abolish slavery linked the Haitian Revolution to emancipation on the mainland, although the new Spanish American republics did not invite Haiti to the <b>Congress of Panama</b> in 1826.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-haiti-1806-1849">Republic of Haiti (1806-1849) · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
